            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"The 'good news' (euangelion) of salvation, the forgiveness of sins, and the inauguration of the Kingdom of God through the incarnation, life, atoning death, and resurrection of Jesus Christ.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"1 Corinthians 15:1-4\",\"Mark 1:14-15\",\"Romans 1:16\",\"Isaiah 52:7\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"A literary genre representing the four canonical written accounts (Matthew, Mark, Luke, John) of the earthly life and ministry of Jesus.\",\"basis\":[\"Mark 1:1\",\"Luke 1:1-4\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"The prophetic and eschatological announcement of liberation, justice, and holistic deliverance for the poor and oppressed.\",\"basis\":[\"Luke 4:18-19\",\"Isaiah 61:1\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"A new law or ethical system based on the teachings of Jesus, such as the Sermon on the Mount.\",\"basis\":[\"Matthew 5:1-7:29\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"While the fundamental definition of 'good news' is highly settled in Christian theology, the emphasis often fluctuates between the Pauline focus on justification by faith through Christ's death and resurrection, and the Synoptic focus on the arrival of the Kingdom of God.\"}",
